WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.063
Good afternoon,
everyone, and Thank

00:00:09.063 --> 00:00:10.230
you very much for having me.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.930
I'm sorry not to be with
you in Washington DC,

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.390
but joining you virtually.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:19.320
And so, I'll kick on,
talking about scaling

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.965
and automating our workflows
with Kubernetes and Airflow.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.160
I spent a lot of my childhood
and teenage years working

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.510
on farms, and any of you
who will have done the same

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.820
will recognize baler twine.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.280
Now the joke goes that you
can solve 90% of your problems

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.500
on a farm with baler twine,
and this is essentially

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.670
nylon string that you'll cut.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:53.700
There's an off cut from or
kind of a byproduct of tying up

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.860
bales of hay, but
it ends up being

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.990
a useful tool in tying
together, say, a farm gate

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:09.820
or trailer to an ATV or
even a lead for your dog.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.870
It's something that's lying
around that's tough and sturdy

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.740
and largely gets the job done.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.960
And the equivalent, I
guess, would be duct tape.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.340
And as you can see and as many
of you will have experienced,

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.740
duct tape isn't really
the appropriate tool

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.810
to get the job done
here or in many cases,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.700
but it sort of works
and it's sort of OK

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.140
up to a certain point.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.150
But then the farm
analogy really is

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.852
that you can get so far with
baler twine, but at some point

00:01:32.852 --> 00:01:34.560
you're going to need
real infrastructure,

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.480
you're going to need
bricks and cement.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.900
And so that's what I'm going
to be talking about today.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.220
So who am I?

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.460
I'm an infectious
disease epidemiologist

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.500
working at the UK
Health Security agency,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.900
or what used to be called
Public Health England.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:54.330
And like many of you, I imagine
at the RStudio conference

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.150
today, you'll have
been an analyst

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.980
of some sort in a
particular specialist area.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.300
And as you've fallen presumably
further in love with R,

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.180
you've ended up becoming more
of a proper data scientist,

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.320
as it would have been back then.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.257
And then you and, I think
this will be a familiar story

00:02:11.257 --> 00:02:12.840
to other that people
have gone through,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.970
or the people will hope
to be going through,

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.670
or will inevitably find
themselves going through,

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:22.200
is finding themselves not having
quite the right tools either

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.440
for their specific area or more
likely just end up, becoming more

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.120
of a software
engineer as they're

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.450
trying to create the tools
for themselves and colleagues

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.750
to work.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.550
And then once you've
got those tools.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.070
And as we heard
in the last talk,

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.800
you're often it's not the
tools so much that are lacking,

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:38.680
but the data.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.610
And so you end up finding
yourself, spending

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.590
all your life
moving data around,

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.610
cleaning it, linking
it, preparing it.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.240
And then you may as
well be a data engineer.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.790
And then the final
stage of transformation,

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.790
as you get further and
further away from analysis,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.760
is when people start to presume
you're a member of your ICT

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.340
department and you
fancilly decided

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.470
to refer to yourself
as a DevOps engineer.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.230
Now this all boils
down to, in my case,

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.390
having a profound love for
both epidemiology and for code.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:12.090
But it's taken,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:17.370
a lot for me to
find this area where

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:24.450
I am in now, which is enablement
of my colleagues and I to,

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.620
analyze epidemiology and
infectious disease data

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:29.910
at scale.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.710
So what am I going
to be talking about?

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:33.610
Exactly that.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:37.200
So how we replaced our
baler twine like solutions

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.940
are they sort of
work, but we need

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.160
them to be a bit
better solutions

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.530
with true infrastructure and,

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.120
clicking on.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.160
I'm going to be talking
about those main tools

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.250
that we've ended up using.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.520
So why Kubernetes and what even
is Kubernetes at this point?

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.910
And then what do we
mean by automation

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.150
and why is it valuable?

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.810
And then Airflow and
why it is actually

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:03.140
and why it's solved so
many of our problems.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:04.730
So the context for
all of this, which

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:07.147
I imagine will be familiar,
but it's important nonetheless

00:04:07.147 --> 00:04:09.440
to buzz through, is that
public health agencies

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.070
have traditionally worked
with maybe hundreds of cases

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.420
of each infectious disease
they specialize in each year.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.860
These are small data sets.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.029
They're easy to manage for
simple analytical tools

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:24.590
and those traditional
ones and the more

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:26.390
modern ones like R and Python.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.360
So there's never
been an impetus per se

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.810
to begin to think about
scale or automation is

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.710
you're not working at
breakneck pace per se.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:40.250
And the turning point
for this in recent years

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.700
has been that both
data that is captured

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.420
and the answers that we're
required to provide to decision

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.790
makers and health protection
colleagues on the ground

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:52.500
is becoming more detailed
and more complex.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.820
And in the UK, Thanks to
our National Health Service,

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:02.120
Health Service, where care
is free and standardized

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:05.480
to a certain extent and we have
collected fantastic volumes

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.060
and quality of
data from it, we're

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.460
needing to understand
it in much more detail

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.460
than we ever had before.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.690
So scale has started to
creep in, but of course

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.410
the - clicking on with my slides -

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.060
We're at this point
where we're needing

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.010
to do more complex
analysis, and so we're

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.550
using more appropriate
tools for moving away

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.560
from those kind of legacy
tools that many of us

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.960
would have been taught in our
infectious disease courses

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.300
at University for those
epidemiologists in the room,

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.130
but are quickly
becomes the lingua

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.020
franca for our topic area.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.850
It's open source,
it's accessible

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.590
and it's usable to
all of us, and it

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.080
answers all the questions
that we needed to.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.720
Now, COVID is
where we've started

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.720
to blend both that
much more complex data

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.680
requirements into needing to
work at unprecedented scale,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.130
going from hundreds of
cases up to millions

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:02.140
of recorded cases in the
UK or confirmed laboratory

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.170
confirmed cases.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:10.290
And so we've needed to move
away from those traditional ways

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:10.870
of working.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.860
And so some people will
say, well, just use SQL

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.280
and you'll be fine.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.250
But as we'll all
know, SQL will only

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.140
take you a certain
way before you

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:23.340
need to be able to wrangle
your data in a much more

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.490
flexible and accessible way.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.900
And so although ODBC
powered workloads

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.560
kind of creep in to increase to
your script and your processes,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.790
really just need to be
able to do R, but bigger.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.760
And that's where
we come to today.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.095
Now the final context is that
and all of our colleagues

00:06:41.095 --> 00:06:42.720
who work in the public
sector will know

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.360
is that you have a
requirement to work

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.900
with high value for money
and have it maintained

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.100
by rather small, small teams,
the small but mighty teams that

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.240
exist in many public
sector organizations.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.960
And then also you're expected
to perform at least the industry

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.680
standard, if not further ahead.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.740
And all of that leads to
quite strong innovation

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.900
in many cases, it's
really a sink or swim,

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.360
and that's really the, COVID was
the sink or swim moment for us.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.930
Now in the UK we're very
lucky in that the UK

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.010
government prefers open source.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.113
It's a written down,

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:24.770
kind of truth that in order
to be achieving high value

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:26.720
for money, you need to
be using open source

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.070
technology where possible.

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.110
And so that was really
quite a nice gateway

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:33.140
into us using these
fantastic tools.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.870
So where have we come from?

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:37.400
We've come from
a place of people

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:41.840
running an individual script, in
a standard operating procedure,

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.290
and then maybe thinking, OK,
I'm going to write all of this

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:47.150
out instead and run
it on a scheduled

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:48.860
task on my laptop
for a Windows users

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:50.900
or a cron job for
our Unix users.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.460
Setting up the scheduled
task on the virtual machine.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.850
When your laptop started
to feel a little bit robust

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.980
if needed to have your
laptop on all the time.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.777
And that's really the beginning
of where the baler twine

00:08:00.777 --> 00:08:02.360
solution start to
creep in, and you're

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:05.090
finding yourself wrapping more
and more of your solutions

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.695
in bits of tough nylon string,
trying to keep it together.

00:08:08.695 --> 00:08:10.070
And that's when
you need to start

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.620
pushing into having
that place of having

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.300
more robust infrastructure.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:18.740
So you might set up a cron job
on an older tool like Slurm,

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:22.620
or if in more modern
cases on Kubernetes.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.670
And then finally getting to
that true infrastructure,

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:27.200
having both an
orchestration tool

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.520
and a compute tool of
having your DAGs in Airflow

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:32.730
running on Kubernetes.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.059
And I'll explain what
those mean at the moment.

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:37.049
So what is Kubernetes?

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:38.190
What are we talking about?

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.100
So Kubernetes first
is open source.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.059
It's a Cloud Native
Computing Foundation

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:48.770
tool that's become pervasive
in running typically

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:53.180
web apps, but now increasingly
data science workloads

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:54.410
across the world.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:58.125
Now the use of
container images moving

00:08:58.125 --> 00:08:59.750
away from well it
works from my machine

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:02.060
to therefore shipping the
entire operating system

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.680
and all of your system packages.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:05.657
And those container
images popularized

00:09:05.657 --> 00:09:07.490
by docker, which everyone
will have heard of

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.615
and they're slightly less
popular, but increasingly

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:13.070
upcoming podman tool providing
very similar functionality.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:17.240
And Kubernetes extends that
idea of running your workloads

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:18.600
in container images.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.680
And instead of having those
container images running

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.040
on your laptop, they
allow you to orchestrate

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:28.110
having those container
images run anywhere.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:30.495
So you could have
Kubernetes on your laptop,

00:09:30.495 --> 00:09:31.620
you could have it anywhere.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:32.995
And I come onto
that in a moment.

00:09:32.995 --> 00:09:35.960
But you specify your software,
your operating system

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.490
in your container
image, and then

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:40.680
you apply resources,
your compute your memory,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.110
your storage into in your
pods and run those together.

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:48.830
Now, pods are entirely
isolated, which is either

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.470
very helpful or not helpful
at all, in which case

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:54.020
you can configure them to not
be isolated from each other

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.080
very specifically.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.150
But it provides that great deal
of security and reassurance

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:03.020
that your workloads can
coexist in whatever environment

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.370
that you have created.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.620
Now the UK Health
Security Agency,

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:10.940
we prefer the Red Hat flavor of
Kubernetes known as OpenShift,

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.690
and I'll talk a little bit
about that now because it's

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:17.270
easy to use
continuous integration

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:18.300
and continuous delivery.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.730
It has those add-ons
and I think everyone,

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:23.660
particularly those are
RStudio on our posit fans

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:26.150
will know that using
open source tools

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:27.540
is certainly cost effective.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.930
But there does
become a time where

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:33.860
paying for the expertise
and the tools that

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:37.550
are enabled, enable the easier
use of those open source tools

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.500
is really worthwhile,
and that's certainly

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:42.050
the case with OpenShift
in our opinion.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:45.710
So that continuous integration,
continuous deployment

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:49.130
is more of a DevOps term, but
to the user, what's important

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:52.760
is that you can have your GitLab
or GitHub repository containing

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:56.360
your code, and whenever you
push your master branch,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:00.890
say, that code is replicated
into your container

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:03.920
image in Kubernetes or
OpenShift ready to go

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:06.080
running just as it
would do on your laptop

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:10.550
and allowing you to push
that workload into whatever

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:12.620
literal environment you want.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.670
Now why have we used it?

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:16.940
And that's primarily
because you want

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.760
to use fewer resources,
because instead

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:23.420
of having a virtual machine per
task configured just the way

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:27.260
you like it, you can figure
for each of your workloads

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:29.520
exactly the operating system.

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:32.990
The system packages your code
in any of your connections

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:34.290
all into one bundle.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.320
And you can put
that onto one place

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:42.620
and then use fewer volumes
of compute and storage

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:45.420
capacity, which is going
to be saving money.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.260
So increasing that value
for money as a start-up,

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:50.720
it's also easier to manage
for ICT administrators

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.380
and I think all of us will
feel either hamstrung or very

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:56.360
sorry for our ICT
colleagues, particularly

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:59.090
in the public sector,
where you want

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:01.880
to make their life as easy
as possible so they can make

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:03.390
your life as easy as possible.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.560
And having all of your workloads
standardized or at least

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:09.560
with a standard
definition, is incredibly

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:13.430
helpful for the simplify
management of those resources.

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:15.680
And the other great thing
about Kubernetes, as well as

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:19.280
it being open source, is that
it runs anywhere. You can have

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:21.890
it available in the public
cloud, like Azure

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:24.530
or GCP, in the hybrid
cloud, if you're

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:27.320
from a larger organization
or on premises

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:31.350
or even on your own laptop
when you're doing development.

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:34.770
And then Kubernetes,
as I said, is frankly

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.320
already the way forward
or increasingly pervasive

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.360
for running containerized web
applications and microservices.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.720
And although data science
is lagging behind,

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:47.190
it's got a lot of
tools and projects

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:53.760
that are propelling into being
more and more commonplace.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.200
So then Airflow, the
second piece of our puzzle

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:59.280
or the second piece of our true
infrastructure solution

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:03.380
and again is open source from
the Apache Software Foundation.

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:06.840
It's a workflow scheduler,
which means that you

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:08.770
it's similar to a cron job.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.640
And one of the funniest ways
of ever being described as it

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.400
was when it was described as
Apache "Fancy cron" as opposed

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.320
to Apache Airflow or
maybe Cron++.

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:20.220
Because it gives you not just
scheduling something to say,

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:27.150
I want to run this command
at 7 AM every day,

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:28.360
seven days a week.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.200
You can say not
only that, but also

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:33.510
email me when there's
a problem or I

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.300
want to see all of
the details of how

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.850
which days it succeeded,
which days it failed,

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:41.560
how many times it
tried to retry itself.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:42.880
And so on and so forth.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:46.230
It's just got those added
extras which give you,

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:51.780
instead of having a
myriad of tasks and custom

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:54.270
scripts with their own
monitoring and logging in them,

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:56.020
Airflow offloads that.

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:58.380
So anything that you
can offload all of that

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.725
thought to Airflow itself.

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:08.500
Now you define your workflows
in directed acyclic graphs

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:10.650
or DAGs as more
commonly referred to.

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:12.370
And now DAGs is not
an uncommon phrase

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:13.640
to those in data science.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:17.350
If any of you use any of you
use the targets R package,

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:19.870
you define your
work in a DAG also

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:21.820
those who work in machine
learning quite often

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:22.670
will have this.

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:25.120
But for those who
don't know, a DAG

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:28.780
is simply a set of tasks and
the relationships between tasks

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:31.300
that show the dependencies
between one thing

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:33.710
having happened and the
next thing having happened.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:37.000
And Airflow manages all
of its workloads in DAGs.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:39.880
Now DAGs and Airflow are
very simple because you have

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:41.990
a simple code as configuration.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:44.287
Although it's written
in Python to an R

00:14:44.287 --> 00:14:46.120
user who hadn't written
any Python before he

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:47.270
started using Airflow,

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:51.670
it's remarkably simple, so don't
let that certainly put you off.

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:54.340
And then this allows
you to do anything

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:56.860
from as simple as
running an R script, just

00:14:56.860 --> 00:15:00.550
an arbitrary, arbitrarily
defined hour script to setting

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:04.870
up Airflow to be
linked to your Hadoop

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.330
cluster or any
other large compute

00:15:07.330 --> 00:15:11.720
cluster or other
data processing tool.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.200
Now, Airflow is
incredibly flexible,

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:16.030
which is fantastic
because it fits

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:17.810
a wide variety of use cases.

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:20.200
But at UKHSA
we've defined, to define that

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.570
into a very
opinionated setup, not

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:25.270
making use of much
of that flexibility

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:29.380
and instead trading that
for a user friendly simple

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:33.710
to learn state, which I'll go
into detail more about now.

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:35.235
So how have we use
these together?

00:15:35.235 --> 00:15:36.610
How have we melded
these together

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:38.170
and why is that useful?

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:42.070
So each team has its own
Kubernetes namespace, so-called,

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:45.070
or a project if you're using
OpenShift specific language

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:50.320
and that allows you to define
users, resources, security

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:53.590
and other details about
all of your workloads

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:56.500
that mean you can have
tenants, different teams

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:57.500
within the organization.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:01.790
And many, both public sector
and private sector teams,

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:03.790
will know that not everyone
in your organization

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:04.957
should see all of your data.

00:16:04.957 --> 00:16:06.490
You've often got
to specify it down,

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.680
so having those
individual tenants

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:10.780
within your organization
is incredibly helpful

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:13.030
and also just allows you to
make sure no one's getting

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:16.930
in their own way or getting
in each other's way even.

00:16:16.930 --> 00:16:20.470
And then in, when you integrate
that with Airflow, you

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:23.500
have all of your container
images, all of your code,

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:27.190
all of credentials,
all set up in air,

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:29.960
in OpenShift or Kubernetes.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:34.520
And then you assign
each DAG to a project.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:38.200
So a team or a team in their
own project or namespace

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.810
can have multiple
DAGs, but each tag only

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.280
looks at it only belongs
to a certain team.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:45.850
And that's been remarkably
helpful as well for making sure

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:50.470
people aren't confused by which
workload is failing, which one

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:52.630
isn't, if it's theirs or not.

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:54.910
And then teams
therefore can only

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.040
see their own project
and their own DAGs

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:59.860
or their own projects and
OpenShift and their own DAGs

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:04.004
in Airflow, which again helps
keep things simple and secure.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.190
PowerPoint letting me down.

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:14.750
And then within that we say
every Airflow task rather than

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:17.240
each different task being
a different operator

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:18.690
as they're known in Airflow.

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:23.900
Each task simply creates a new
pod in Kubernetes and OpenShift

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:26.300
with your container image,
with all of your code,

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:28.517
and then you give it
a specific command.

00:17:28.517 --> 00:17:30.350
And most often in my
workloads, that command

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:32.630
is simply to run an
R script, because we

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:38.330
offload all of the connections
to our other resources

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:41.600
or secrets credentials
to Kubernetes,

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:49.100
where that enterprise
level security and resource

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.070
management happens.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:52.910
And then Airflow is
simply telling Kubernetes

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:56.540
how to run a pod and
with what resources.

00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:00.170
And this is what we found
to be after a lot of battle

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:03.530
testing and user acceptance
to be the most simple,

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:07.940
secure and easy to manage way
to move workloads from people

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:10.790
running individual
scripts on their laptops

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:13.238
into Kubernetes and
Airflow, which saves,

00:18:13.238 --> 00:18:14.780
as you can already
imagine, you don't

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:17.780
need me to spell out vast
amounts of staff time

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:22.430
and also observability,
making sure

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.010
that the entire team knows
when something's happened

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:28.790
rather than needing to send
teams or Slack message to ask,

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.040
have you run the
script for today?

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:35.000
So the important event is
how do you get started?

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:38.450
Well, if you haven't
already got started,

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:42.440
you can use the popular
and although not open

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.840
source kind of in
the case of docker,

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:47.660
but tools like Docker and
podman to write and build

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:53.330
your container images and then
your or working containers

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:56.480
to start with a tool, now
Red Hat with its commitment

00:18:56.480 --> 00:19:00.240
to open source allow provides
these free developer licenses,

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.240
which means that you can
have your own OpenShift

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:05.000
cluster on your laptop,
which is the perfect way

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.880
to get started with learning
how the system, how Kubernetes

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:11.670
works itself with these user
friendly, easy to get started

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:15.950
add-ons that the OpenShift
flavor of Kubernetes provides.

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:18.240
Airflow, as we said,
was open source.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:19.890
It's simply a Python package.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:21.980
You can pip install
it very happily

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:23.810
and then begin
playing with your,

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:26.380
with simple DAGs
in half an hour.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.920
And then when you want to go
to the next step, how to use

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:34.740
or using OpenShift
Kubernetes yourself,

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:36.270
you're using Airflow yourself.

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:38.250
It's then time to instead
of having Airflow

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.980
on your own computer
put onto Kubernetes,

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:45.090
you have the official
prebuilt container

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:47.790
images to run
Airflow on Kubernetes

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:49.390
or on your own computer.

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:52.230
And then after that, when
you've got your workloads

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:54.660
with small amounts of
resources on your own laptop,

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:57.090
you can say, OK, I'm
ready now to move on

00:19:57.090 --> 00:19:59.320
to the main Kubernetes
cluster.

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:02.820
And that takes you from
the 8-16 gigabytes of memory

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.990
and 8 CPU that have on a laptop up
to as large as you can possibly

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.930
achieve with the hardware
that we currently

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:13.630
have in terms of your compute.

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:16.370
Now extra tools that you
might want to know about.

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:18.670
There are a couple that
will really help you

00:20:18.670 --> 00:20:21.850
in this kind of very high
level summary of this massively

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:24.340
complicated topic,
both the rocker project

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:27.190
providing container images
with R installed and ready

00:20:27.190 --> 00:20:28.050
to use or so.

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:29.800
And I'm not just saying
this because we're

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:31.570
speaking at the R
RStudio conference,

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:35.560
our studio's public version
of its Package Manager

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:38.710
has been enormously
both time saving

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:42.010
and helpful for having
R package binaries

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:44.663
for Linux available
to the public.

00:20:44.663 --> 00:20:47.080
It's a truly wonderful resource
which I couldn't recommend

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:49.780
highly enough, but then
actually if you're just

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:53.170
wanting to get started with
Kubernetes, not in a production

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:55.330
sense, but in terms of
doing your own development,

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:57.610
the absolute best
way to be learning

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:01.300
and to moving your development
workloads into Kubernetes,

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:04.750
into the cloud is using RStudio
Workbench, which I couldn't

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:05.892
endorse more thoroughly.

00:21:05.892 --> 00:21:07.600
And I know that I've
come up to time now,

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:09.230
so I'm going to hold there.

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:12.180
And Thank you very
much for listening.

